1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,137
On the frozen sea ice, a hunter kneels and cuts square blocks from hard snow.

2
00:00:06,387 --> 00:00:11,866
The wind that numbs his fingers is the same material he is about to live inside.

3
00:00:12,716 --> 00:00:15,799
Shelter from the cold, built out of the cold.

4
00:00:16,049 --> 00:00:22,898
This is how some Inuit hunters in the Canadian Arctic and northern Greenland turned snow into a winter hunting shelter,

5
00:00:23,148 --> 00:00:27,258
not a year-round home, and not a technique every Arctic people used.

6
00:00:28,108 --> 00:00:31,190
Snow looks like the enemy of warmth, cold, wet,

7
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:34,522
everywhere you don't want to be stuck in winter.

8
00:00:35,372 --> 00:00:37,427
But snow isn't the real problem.

9
00:00:37,677 --> 00:00:38,877
Heat loss is.

10
00:00:39,127 --> 00:00:42,894
Warmth doesn't vanish on its own; it leaks toward anything colder.

11
00:00:43,144 --> 00:00:46,226
The real question isn't how to beat the cold.

12
00:00:46,476 --> 00:00:48,873
It's what stops the heat from leaving.

13
00:00:49,723 --> 00:00:51,436
Snow is mostly trapped air.

14
00:00:51,686 --> 00:00:56,823
Pack a handful and most of what you're holding is empty space between ice crystals,

15
00:00:57,073 --> 00:00:59,812
space that doesn't let heat move through easily.

16
00:01:00,662 --> 00:01:03,402
It's the same idea behind a down jacket,

17
00:01:03,652 --> 00:01:08,104
or a window with two panes and a gap of air between them.

18
00:01:08,954 --> 00:01:12,379
Fresh snow conducts heat about as slowly as still air.

19
00:01:12,629 --> 00:01:15,368
Solid ice conducts heat around seventy times faster.

20
00:01:15,618 --> 00:01:20,755
That difference is the whole trick: a wall built from packed snow holds warmth in,

21
00:01:21,005 --> 00:01:25,800
while the same wall built from solid ice would let it pour straight through.

22
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:31,102
Builders cut blocks straight from hard snowpack and stack them in a spiral,

23
00:01:31,352 --> 00:01:35,804
each ring leaning slightly inward until the dome seals shut at the top.

24
00:01:36,054 --> 00:01:41,534
Inside, the air warms from body heat alone, melting a thin inner layer of the walls.

25
00:01:41,784 --> 00:01:44,866
That meltwater refreezes into a thin shell of ice,

26
00:01:45,116 --> 00:01:48,540
locking the blocks together and making the whole dome stronger.

27
00:01:49,390 --> 00:01:53,500
Exactly how warm it gets inside depends on the snow, the wind,

28
00:01:53,750 --> 00:01:56,832
and the weather outside, there's no single fixed number.

29
00:01:57,682 --> 00:02:01,449
Back on the sea ice, that hunter isn't just cutting blocks,

30
00:02:01,699 --> 00:02:06,494
he's building a wall of trapped air to keep his own warmth from escaping.

31
00:02:06,744 --> 00:02:11,881
Your coat does the same job tonight, and so does the glass in your window.

32
00:02:12,131 --> 00:02:14,186
Warmth doesn't need walls of stone.

33
00:02:14,436 --> 00:02:16,833
It just needs air that can't move.
